WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:01.660
Hey, watch out.

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:07.870
If you don't want spoilers, turn this video
off now, maybe watch it later after you see

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.919
the movie.

00:00:10.919 --> 00:00:15.690
I'm here with Tom and Nina to talk about best
and worst things we saw in Black Widow.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:20.800
Nina, what are the best and worst things you
saw in Black Widow?

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.460
Thanks Brian.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.740
Well, my gut answer is just to say Florence
Pugh, because I love her.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.039
I thought she was really good in this movie.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:29.039
I really liked...

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:30.039
So that's your worst?

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:31.109
No, that's my best.

00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:33.680
I was just kidding, I know that's best.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.850
I was just being a jerk.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.059
I mean, I could be flip and just say Florence
Pugh, because she's really good as Yelena,

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:43.789
and as I'm sure we'll discuss, I think she
probably has a future within the MCU as well,

00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:49.770
and her just really dead pan monotonous dry
wit, was a welcome addition.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.519
But I also feel like I need to say, the whole
casting of the family was very good.

00:00:54.519 --> 00:01:00.620
David Harbour was really funny, Alexei was
a great addition and I loved seeing Rachel

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:01.690
Weisz in something.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.010
That's not an Oscar bait...

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.010
Oscar bait.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.010
... super dramatic biopic.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.010
Yeah.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:07.010
Jinx, jinx.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:11.740
Yeah, it was nice to see Daniel Craig's main
squeeze having some fun.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:17.320
And I loved the dynamic and the chemistry
between all four of the found family assassins.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:23.420
Now that you mention Rachel Weisz having fun,
it just reminds me that one of her first big

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.060
roles was in The Mummy where she plays this
great...

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.060
Yeah, that's true, yeah.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.371
Like what was it? 1930s era, like she had
the eyebrows and the makeup and everything,

00:01:33.371 --> 00:01:36.690
and she just inhabited this great old time-y
film role.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.560
And she has a great bunch of fun, she's doing
pratfalls, she's doing action.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.330
You know, it's all happening.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:47.160
And so yeah, now that you mention it, it's
like, this is the kind of stuff that she came

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.520
into Hollywood doing.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.040
And she did a great job here too.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.040
Yeah, I totally agree.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.250
The cast was fantastic.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.250
Yeah.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.460
And I feel like when you let an actress like
that have fun and actually another Rachel,

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.530
but I feel like this is true of McAdams, where
once you let her just enjoy herself on screen,

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.730
she really lights up and she makes the whole
movie better.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:07.660
Hopefully she'll do some more interesting
things in Doctor Strange 2.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.660
Yeah, in Doctor Strange, exactly.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.890
My dog has really strong feelings about this,
clearly.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:11.890
Yeah...

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.890
Guys I know that we get off topic like really
easily, but why don't we just really quick

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.120
all name our top 20 favorite Rachel's.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.459
Just off the top of our heads.

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:24.439
Anyway, I loved the chemistry between the
family.

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:27.890
I thought just the smaller moments of the
film, I mean, we talked about this in what

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.670
we wanted to see in Black Widow, and I got
a lot of that.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.280
I got a lot of that emotional core.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.010
I got a real some really fun scenes between
the family members.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:40.530
And then I also got exactly what I didn't
want, which was something really dumb and

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:46.090
Marvel-y, and it was the floating Red Room
station, which is so stupid.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.659
Like why...

00:02:47.659 --> 00:02:48.750
I hate it.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:53.060
I just was like, they needed a dumb, giant
FX thing to put...

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.469
Like it could have been on the ground, it
could have been underground, it could have

00:02:55.469 --> 00:02:56.469
been literally anywhere.

00:02:56.469 --> 00:03:05.120
And they were like, let's make it a spacecraft,
or a spacecraft adjacent thing.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:06.890
It's the Soviet Helicarrier.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:07.890
It was stupid, Brian.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.489
I stand by it.

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:12.480
I'm going to jump off your worst into my worst.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:13.480
That's fine.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:19.680
I don't necessarily agree that the floating
Red Room was the worst part, I didn't mind

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:20.680
it.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.680
I thought that was fine.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.680
I had no problem with it.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:29.219
My problem was more with the villain whose
name I have already forgotten, played by Ray

00:03:29.219 --> 00:03:30.219
Winston, Winstone?Winston?

00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:31.459
We'll go with Winston.

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:32.580
Oh my God.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.629
I forgot it too and I re-watched the movie
this morning.

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:40.830
He's not a great villain because I don't really
know what he did, or what he wanted or what

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.829
power he had.

00:03:42.829 --> 00:03:47.150
He had that big monologue at the end where
he's like, "I have Black Widows all over the

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:48.890
world."

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:49.890
That's my bad impression.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:50.890
Spot on.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:51.890
Thank you.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:52.890
He has Black Widows all over the world...

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:53.890
Great Russian accent.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:54.890
Terrific.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.890
And he...

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:56.890
See, I didn't commit that's the problem, I
didn't commit.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.159
That's the problem, I didn't commit.

00:03:58.159 --> 00:03:59.749
You should've played that part.

00:03:59.749 --> 00:04:01.680
It was like I was back in the Kremlin.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.079
But he's talking about how his...

00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:04.809
Is this the The Yalta Conference?

00:04:04.809 --> 00:04:08.359
... He's got all these Black Widows all over
the world, and he's talking about how he has

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:12.879
all this power, but it's never clear what
that power is, how he actually wields it,

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:16.250
what the consequences are of him continuing
to wield it.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:21.290
And I feel like it's reach kind of exceeded
its grasp there, where it's trying to make

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.470
him a big, bad Marvel global villain.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:31.480
But at the end of the day, he's just kind
of like a very generic, evil jerk with no

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:32.480
clear deal.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.260
But I feel like they did explain what power
he had, where he's like, "I have sleeper agents

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.700
all over the world who can do whatever I want,
whenever I want at any given moment."

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.960
Yeah they explain it, but to what end?

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.850
Like, they don't show it.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:44.850
He just says it.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:45.850
Sure, sure.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:47.040
He's just like, "Oh, did you know?

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.230
I have assassins all over the world."

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.160
And we're like, "Oh, interesting."

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.350
And then the movie goes, "Well now you don't."

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:54.350
So it's just...

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.140
So in that vain, just quickly I also found
the Taskmaster reveal really underwhelming,

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.790
having to do with the villain.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.400
I didn't mind that I was...

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.820
We all predicted that it was going to be Rachel
Weisz.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:05.820
And then it wasn't.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:06.820
I'm glad it wasn't actually.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:07.820
I don't think any of us said that out loud.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.950
I don't think any of us were very adamant
that that was what was going to happen.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:11.950
Let's cut directly to that.

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:14.630
Do we have footage of that?

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:19.900
Potential spoiler alert, she predicts that
it's going to be Rachel Weisz's character.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.540
Of course it's going to be Rachel 
Weisz's character.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:23.540
Okay.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.910
Do we all pretty much think that this is what's
happening?

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:26.910
Are we children?

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.710
I'm sorry I thought that we were having an
adult conversation.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.420
I was happy to see that it wasn't her.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:33.480
I was too.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.700
And I didn't mind the reveal, see I thought
that worked really well because overall this

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:41.780
is one of the things I really liked about
the movie, which is that this is a movie that's

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:42.780
about something for once.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:48.200
Maybe not for once, but you know what I mean,
like this has a very clear theme going from

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:54.370
start to finish where this is about kind of
confronting your trauma from your past and

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:59.530
trying to resolve it, trying to accept it,
come to grips it, and resolve it and move

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:00.530
on.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:06.320
And so the reveal of the Taskmaster was actually
the little girl that Natasha thought she had

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.240
murdered in her attempt to get out.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:15.630
Doing something to fix her trauma, means exiting,
defecting, but she had to kill this girl to

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:16.630
do it.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.080
And then we find out that the girl is alive...

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.760
It all works really well for me.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.550
No, you've kind of turned me around on it
to be honest.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:27.240
No you did, I'm not being sarcastic.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.530
I guess I didn't think about it in the framework
of the overarching message of the movie's

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.920
approach to how you handle trauma.

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:38.121
I just think at the time I was like, "Well,
I don't really know who this person is," but

00:06:38.121 --> 00:06:41.990
I think upon a re-watch or something like
that, I think you're right.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:43.360
It does tie into the theme of the movie really
well.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.210
I mean, it's obviously...

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.240
Yeah, go ahead.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:50.580
I'm I'm a little bit disappointed to hear
that you don't think that the other Marvel

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:51.580
movies had messages.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.340
I mean, I think that like they've always been
very socially conscious and had like kind

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:57.990
of a political undertone to them.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:02.930
I feel like Ironman 2 kind of had that message
about like, drones are bad.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:07.060
And then Age of Ultron, they had that message
about, you don't want to build drones because

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.060
they're bad.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:12.350
And then Captain America and The Winter Soldier,
there was that whole plot line about how drones

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:15.440
aren't very good and shouldn't be employed.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.930
It's always been kind of a rainbow of messages
to me.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:22.670
You know, I hadn't considered that and I appreciate
you pointing that out.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.870
The drone centric messaging of the MCU.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:26.930
I'm a very smart man.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:31.620
Was there a similar theme at work in Guardians
of the Galaxy Volume 2 about drones?

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:35.900
They're fun to kill, I think that was part
of it, but I don't recall.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.730
Whereas in this one, it was very much about
how you shouldn't take women and turn them

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:40.780
into drones.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.980
You shouldn't take women and turn them into
drones.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:43.980
That's true.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.640
I hadn't even considered that theme.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.260
So, that's exciting.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.860
Yeah, life lessons With Tom.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:53.100
I'm the Mr. Rogers of a generation.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.150
That's what the tie is all about, you should
be wearing a sweater.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:56.150
Next time.

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:57.810
It's 112 degrees here, Brian.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:58.810
Oh yeah that's right, sorry.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:00.710
I'm very much ready to die.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:02.800
Apologies, shouldn't have laughed at that.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:03.920
No.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.520
So I feel like overall yeah, that was one
of my best things that it had a really great

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:09.520
arc.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.330
I had a great theme, but I feel like the ultimate
villain reveal for me very much fell flat.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:16.660
It was super forgettable.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:21.460
The stakes were high enough with, "We have
to free all these women.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:25.370
I have to deal with my family and figure this
out.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:26.480
I have to kill this guy."

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.600
It would have been enough to kill a bad guy
who was abducting women and turning them into

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:32.600
assassins.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:38.620
I think for me, it would have been more interesting
if he was not this all powerful secret guy.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:44.099
And he was more explicitly like, "I have these
assassins all over the world and I sell them

00:08:44.099 --> 00:08:45.759
to the highest bidder.

00:08:45.759 --> 00:08:50.019
Influence geopolitical events, and I don't
care what happens as long as I get my money

00:08:50.019 --> 00:08:52.850
and get to live in my floating secret base."

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:58.050
Like all of those things I think would have
made for a more interesting explanation.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:01.339
And then Natasha's just like, "Well that ends
now, and I'm going to kill..."

00:09:01.339 --> 00:09:04.870
The stakes were high enough where she needs
to kill this guy, she needs to stop this program

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:12.589
and free these women, making it this secret
world, spider web, pun intended, of Black

00:09:12.589 --> 00:09:16.870
Widows for me it didn't feel necessary.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:20.449
So it kind of felt a little tacked on.

00:09:20.449 --> 00:09:27.939
My other least favorite thing was the song
under the opening credit, the awful cover

00:09:27.939 --> 00:09:29.820
of Smells Like Teen Spirit.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:33.220
Tom, did you want to say something about that?

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:36.720
Just like four or five paragraphs.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.829
Go ahead.

00:09:38.829 --> 00:09:40.949
Totally fine.

00:09:40.949 --> 00:09:44.740
It really bummed me out to hear that cover
of Smells Like Teen Spirit.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:49.509
I mean, I'm not going to pull some kind of
old man card and say, "Oh, that song should

00:09:49.509 --> 00:09:50.509
be left alone."

00:09:50.509 --> 00:09:52.370
I don't care about any of that.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:53.529
Like, it's a good song.

00:09:53.529 --> 00:09:54.579
It's a fine song.

00:09:54.579 --> 00:10:02.129
But when that song started playing, I could
not help but think how lame and silly it was.

00:10:02.129 --> 00:10:07.160
Thankfully once it was over the movie kicked
into gear and I really enjoy pretty much everything

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:08.160
that came after it.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.500
Overall, I really liked this movie.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.050
I did too, yeah.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:14.279
But that definitely didn't bode well.

00:10:14.279 --> 00:10:17.510
I was going to say, what a way to kick it
off.

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:18.510
Right?

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:21.459
Because we're all aging right in front of
everybody's eyes.

00:10:21.459 --> 00:10:24.009
We all existed during the nineties, right?

00:10:24.009 --> 00:10:28.529
We remember what it was like to pretend that
things had depth, so that people would like

00:10:28.529 --> 00:10:29.529
us more.

00:10:29.529 --> 00:10:35.490
But you're right, Smells Like Teen Spirit,
if I've got to pretend for one more day that

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:40.491
the lyrics of that song have any emotional
depth to them at all, I am going to burn every

00:10:40.491 --> 00:10:42.610
Land's End factory outlet to the ground.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:47.230
I think that we have trusted this country
with flannel for far too long and look at

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:50.449
what we've done with it.

00:10:50.449 --> 00:10:53.370
You're part of the problem, Brian, be a part
of the solution.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:54.370
I'm triggered.

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:58.029
I like this shirt, this is one of my favorite
shirts.

00:10:58.029 --> 00:10:59.149
No, you're right though.

00:10:59.149 --> 00:11:03.069
Like Kurt Cobain has famously said that he
doesn't really put much into his lyrics.

00:11:03.069 --> 00:11:05.689
He just looks for stuff that rhymes and sounds
good.

00:11:05.689 --> 00:11:06.769
You're kidding me.

00:11:06.769 --> 00:11:07.769
Yeah.

00:11:07.769 --> 00:11:08.769
I know.

00:11:08.769 --> 00:11:09.769
Surprise.

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:12.480
You're saying that the moment where he thought
that mosquito and libido sound pretty similar,

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:14.629
that didn't carry a lot of...

00:11:14.629 --> 00:11:19.259
There's only one good on-screen use of Smells
Like Teen Spirit and it is during the cancan

00:11:19.259 --> 00:11:21.249
dance in Moulin Rouge.

00:11:21.249 --> 00:11:23.449
That's it, that's it.

00:11:23.449 --> 00:11:26.610
I don't make the rules, but also I make the
rules.

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:27.610
Okay.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:29.290
That's fair.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:33.259
Tom, what were your best and worst moments
from Black Widow?

00:11:33.259 --> 00:11:37.350
And let me tell you, Brian, I'm glad that
you asked because I think that we can all

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:43.709
agree having been around for a minute that
trying to find the opinions of a 30 year old

00:11:43.709 --> 00:11:47.540
white man on what Scarlett Johansson is doing
professionally on the internet, is like trying

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:49.290
to find water in the desert.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:52.690
So it's good that you came to me with these
questions.

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:55.090
Yeah, Colin Jost wasn't available Tom, so
we had to call you.

00:11:55.090 --> 00:12:02.709
Thank you very much I bring half the charisma
and four times the forehead.

00:12:02.709 --> 00:12:05.180
I agree with a lot of what you guys said.

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:06.889
I thought the cast was terrific.

00:12:06.889 --> 00:12:11.079
I thought that that family dinner scene, it
almost felt like taking a breath for the first

00:12:11.079 --> 00:12:12.570
time in the movie.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:13.570
So dour.

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:14.570
And then you get that one scene...

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:17.579
I also love, just quickly, when Rachel Weisz
is like, "Stop slouching, you're slouching."

00:12:17.579 --> 00:12:18.579
Yeah, terrific.

00:12:18.579 --> 00:12:21.230
That is what every mother says to her child.

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:22.230
Spot on.

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:23.230
Like they nailed that whole dynamic.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:24.439
I thought that was really charming.

00:12:24.439 --> 00:12:25.980
I thought that was delightful.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:30.829
I thought it was maybe a little hasty of the
main characters to assume that the bad guy

00:12:30.829 --> 00:12:31.829
was dead.

00:12:31.829 --> 00:12:33.199
And I agree with you about the bad guy.

00:12:33.199 --> 00:12:38.990
Although, I do think that he brought roughly
the same energy as John Belushi in Blues Brothers

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.430
when he asks "How much for the little girl?"

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:45.890
And that was as far as they got, right.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:50.200
I thought it was hasty, maybe shortsighted
for them to assume that he died in that explosion

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:55.540
since throughout the entire movie, explosions
were treated like sort of a roadblock more

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:56.540
than anything.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:00.309
It's just this brief inconvenience that keeps
happening to people.

00:13:00.309 --> 00:13:04.170
Yeah, Florence Pugh's character there at the
end, like she just stands right next to an

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:09.490
exploding engine and rides that wave right
down to the ground.

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:14.560
That Lonely Island, Cool Guys Don't Look at
Explosions is exactly correct.

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:16.870
Almost exactly, yeah.

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:19.759
Except I think she was staring it right down
the barrel too.

00:13:19.759 --> 00:13:23.850
And then Natasha's car gets exploded like
twice in a row.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.119
So, that was a little bit funky.

00:13:25.119 --> 00:13:30.100
I'm not going to whine about Taskmaster, it
would be cool to see comic book accurate Taskmaster,

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:33.480
but the way that they did all the fight choreography
with that character, that was terrific.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:34.480
Really, really cool.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:37.269
I thought the fight choreography across the
board was really good.

00:13:37.269 --> 00:13:38.410
It's pretty neat.

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:40.529
Like, I really enjoyed watching the fights.

00:13:40.529 --> 00:13:45.029
They didn't feel as sky laser beam-y, as Marvel
tends to.

00:13:45.029 --> 00:13:50.899
I felt like the camera work was very tight
and focused and the fights looked...

00:13:50.899 --> 00:13:56.420
And also good job on the sound editing, like
every punch sounded good.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:58.910
How great was that car chase in Budapest?

00:13:58.910 --> 00:13:59.910
Really good.

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:00.910
Oh my God.

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:02.699
I was laughing like a maniac when all of a
sudden...

00:14:02.699 --> 00:14:04.149
The door maneuver she does?

00:14:04.149 --> 00:14:06.230
Yeah, I loved that.

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:07.429
That was a great scene.

00:14:07.429 --> 00:14:13.480
That brings up another thing, I feel like
they didn't lean too hard into needing to

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:16.559
define everything that they pointed out about
this character.

00:14:16.559 --> 00:14:22.339
Like we didn't get the half-hour flashback
sequence to Budapest, I am really comfortable

00:14:22.339 --> 00:14:23.339
with that.

00:14:23.339 --> 00:14:25.429
Like just let some of it be head canon, that's
fine.

00:14:25.429 --> 00:14:26.429
We got enough, yeah.

00:14:26.429 --> 00:14:27.429
Yeah, right?

00:14:27.429 --> 00:14:29.269
They explained it, but they didn't make it
this big...

00:14:29.269 --> 00:14:30.439
Beat us over the head with it.

00:14:30.439 --> 00:14:31.439
Yeah.

00:14:31.439 --> 00:14:32.970
It was really well done overall.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:38.240
Yeah, no, very much not so low, which is what
I was worried about.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:43.040
I feel like post-quarantine, David Harbour
trying to fit into his pants is what we all

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:44.389
needed to see.

00:14:44.389 --> 00:14:45.420
Yeah, yeah.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.140
Just be convinced that he looks exactly fine.

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:49.140
He was so funny.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:50.660
I loved David Harbour through this whole thing.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.050
He was so good.

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:55.879
It a little bit bummed me out that he didn't
get a better Hellboy, because I think he would

00:14:55.879 --> 00:14:57.649
have done some great stuff with that character.

00:14:57.649 --> 00:14:58.649
He's fantastic.

00:14:58.649 --> 00:15:00.079
He was hilarious.

00:15:00.079 --> 00:15:01.939
Everything he did on screen it made me laugh.

00:15:01.939 --> 00:15:02.939
He was great.

00:15:02.939 --> 00:15:03.939
Yeah.

00:15:03.939 --> 00:15:07.720
And then my only other nitpick is just that,
there's all of this really incongruence technology

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:11.339
stuff that goes on in MCU movies and it's
fun.

00:15:11.339 --> 00:15:16.269
It's like, we've got a flying helicarrier
base that nobody can pick up on radar, but

00:15:16.269 --> 00:15:21.709
the best way that we have to mark our cut
for brain surgery is still just a magic marker.

00:15:21.709 --> 00:15:22.709
Magic marker.

00:15:22.709 --> 00:15:23.709
Yeah.

00:15:23.709 --> 00:15:31.110
I had the same thing in Civil War when Rhodey
gets shot out of the sky, and then they take

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:32.930
him to a hospital to get an MRI.

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:36.129
And I think Vision is like standing there
watching this happen.

00:15:36.129 --> 00:15:39.499
It's like, we've got this living machine whose...

00:15:39.499 --> 00:15:42.670
He's like, "No, I can't do a leg scan, it's
fine."

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:45.060
Just put him in the big magnet.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:48.819
Also, should Vision be anywhere near an MRI
machine?

00:15:48.819 --> 00:15:52.470
He would definitely interfere with a giant
magnet.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:55.499
Very much the same, yep.

00:15:55.499 --> 00:15:57.899
Also, how did we feel about like the...

00:15:57.899 --> 00:16:02.149
So I am a sucker for any movie that features
someone pulling off a face that's not their

00:16:02.149 --> 00:16:03.149
face.

00:16:03.149 --> 00:16:04.149
I love that.

00:16:04.149 --> 00:16:06.250
It's such a good gimmick that gets me every
time.

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:08.570
There were strong Mission impossible vibes.

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:09.570
Yeah.

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.959
And there were strong Americans vibes, one
of my favorite shows.

00:16:12.959 --> 00:16:14.170
So yeah, overall...

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:15.170
Yeah.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:18.040
I liked that reveal that Rachel Weisz and Scarlett
Johansson switched places, I thought that

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:19.040
worked really well.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:20.040
It was great.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:22.670
Once it happened, you're just like, "Oh okay."

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:23.670
Yeah.

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:25.730
And it wasn't some dumb, overly convoluted
explanation.

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:27.249
It was just like, no, they just planned it.

00:16:27.249 --> 00:16:28.249
That's it.

00:16:28.249 --> 00:16:29.519
It was very straightforward, I liked that.

00:16:29.519 --> 00:16:30.519
It was good.

00:16:30.519 --> 00:16:34.660
Managed to do like a flashback, heist explanation
thing without making it seem trite or stupid.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.000
It's just very brief.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.000
Yeah.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.589
No, it was nicely done.

00:16:38.589 --> 00:16:46.999
But on the flip side, let's talk about that
post credit scene, which in my opinion, I

00:16:46.999 --> 00:16:48.889
didn't like it.

00:16:48.889 --> 00:16:50.790
I loved it.

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:51.790
I thought it was so lame.

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:52.790
I liked...

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:53.790
Were you just mad about the knock on the Midwest?

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:54.790
Are you still mad?

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:55.790
Not at all.

00:16:55.790 --> 00:16:56.790
No.

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:57.790
I don't care about that at all.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:16:58.869
I thought it was great when she went to Natasha's...

00:16:58.869 --> 00:17:02.809
I mean, not great that she went to her grave,
but I thought that the scene up to that point

00:17:02.809 --> 00:17:04.559
was good, it was touching, it's effecting.

00:17:04.559 --> 00:17:09.750
I'm a little annoyed that we got a pretty
good movie for Black Widow and now she's dead.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:10.970
So that bums me out, generally.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.530
I hate that, yeah.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:15.110
But whatever, like we'll put that aside.

00:17:15.110 --> 00:17:20.310
Then the Contessa is there, we're still not
a hundred percent sure what she does, who

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:21.570
she is.

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:25.670
We know that Florence Pugh's character Yelena
works for her in some capacity, I guess as

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:27.380
an assassin.

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:29.750
And then she says, "Here's your next target.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:33.519
The person who got your sister killed or killed
your sister."

00:17:33.519 --> 00:17:37.450
And it's a picture of Hawkeye and immediately
I'm like, "Oh, okay.

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:41.300
So you Yelena and Hawkeye will fight, and
then they'll be friends.

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:46.309
Like, there's nothing that's going to happen
that really will surprise me that much because...

00:17:46.309 --> 00:17:48.850
I mean, I wouldn't lose any sleep if she actually
killed him, but you're probably right.

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:49.960
I'm just saying like...

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:55.059
Well yeah, I mean if that happened, that would
be really shocking, but I would...

00:17:55.059 --> 00:17:58.120
Yeah, that's not the MCUs M.O.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:17:59.280
So like for me, a lot of the post credit scenes...

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.390
This isn't seasons one through three of Game
of Thrones, they don't just kill people with

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:03.700
abandon.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:09.690
Most of the other post credit scenes throughout
the MCUs history have all kind of been really

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:14.350
interesting teasers, really great setups for
characters we haven't met or events that we're

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.049
really excited about and interested in.

00:18:16.049 --> 00:18:21.980
But this is like, "Hey, check out this guy,
he's getting his own show on Disney+ soon.

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:23.899
You're going to be in it."

00:18:23.899 --> 00:18:25.770
And it's like, cut to black.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:26.770
Okay.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:29.929
So that's my feeling, but clearly I'm not
the only one who thought that.

00:18:29.929 --> 00:18:33.350
Isn't that what the post credit scenes have
kind of been doing for the last few.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:39.130
Yeah I mean, one of them was Sam Jackson being
like, "Do you want to join the Avengers?"

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:41.850
And then there's a movie that came out called,
The Avengers.

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:46.669
No, wait a second though Nina, that was before
we'd ever seen anything like The Avengers

00:18:46.669 --> 00:18:47.820
before, like that was...

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:48.820
That's true.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:49.820
That was a true tease of something huge...

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:54.309
As long as you don't count 1997s Avengers,
staring Uma Thurman and Ralph Fiennes.

00:18:54.309 --> 00:18:55.309
Oh my God, I forgot about that.

00:18:55.309 --> 00:18:58.750
And I want to say Sean Connery in a giant
Teddy bear costume.

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:01.289
I'm not saying that all credit scenes are
great.

00:19:01.289 --> 00:19:07.070
I'm saying that a lot of them tend to tease
things in interesting, exciting ways.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:10.210
Like, I know a guy, right?

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:11.210
Like they've got Bucky there...

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:13.110
Right, or Thanos being like, "I'll do it myself."

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:14.620
Thanos, I'll do it...

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:18.360
Exactly, big teases where you're like, "Oh,
this hooks into this big storyline I've never

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:19.360
heard."

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:23.410
Or I guess, Fury and Maria disappearing and
the beeper having the Captain Marvel logo.

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:28.429
The Captain Marvel symbol, and then Captain
Marvel coming through at the end of her movie,

00:19:28.429 --> 00:19:29.429
and teased...

00:19:29.429 --> 00:19:34.400
It's like the way that they teased that that
was a thing that was coming up.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.000
I don't understand the complaint.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:36.820
I'm so sorry.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:37.820
I'm so sorry.

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:45.441
I'm saying that those teases in the previous
movies, I felt like hinted at large, interesting

00:19:45.441 --> 00:19:48.890
new characters or ideas.

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:54.590
Whereas this is, "Hey, check out this character,
all the viewers remember from the other movies,

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:56.850
you're going to fight and then you'll be friends."

00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:01.570
Counterpoint, is it a bad reveal because it
involves one of the least interesting MCU

00:20:01.570 --> 00:20:02.570
characters?

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:03.570
Maybe.

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:06.740
I just feel like there's nothing there for
me to sink my teeth into.

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:07.740
It seems like a box...

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:08.740
I feel that way about Hawkeye period.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:09.740
I'm just like, okay...

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:10.740
It seems like a box standard...

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:12.450
a work life balance, do you think a lot of
other heroes would do that?

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:15.559
You'll fight and then you won't fight.

00:20:15.559 --> 00:20:17.429
He's raising a family.

00:20:17.429 --> 00:20:19.640
He married Linda Cardellini, what else do
you want him to do?`

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:22.929
Okay, I will give him kudos for marrying Linda
Cardellini, that's a get, I will give him

00:20:22.929 --> 00:20:23.929
that.

00:20:23.929 --> 00:20:27.649
We've all dreamed of marrying Linda Cardellini,
that goes without saying.

00:20:27.649 --> 00:20:32.139
But anyway, I'm going to shut up because I'm
the only person who...

00:20:32.139 --> 00:20:33.139
It's Lindsay Weir...

00:20:33.139 --> 00:20:34.139
Lindsay Weir, yeah thank you.

00:20:34.139 --> 00:20:37.700
I'm the only person who didn't really get
much out of that post credit scene.

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:38.789
But what did you guys think?

00:20:38.789 --> 00:20:39.789
You obviously liked it.

00:20:39.789 --> 00:20:40.789
Tom, you go ahead.

00:20:40.789 --> 00:20:43.190
I got nothing out of it.

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:47.190
It didn't really do anything for me.

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:52.260
It does bother me that the biggest hook that
a lot of these things have is, "Just wait

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:53.350
until next time.

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:58.409
Man, spend another $12 and you're going to
have an even better time in six months."

00:20:58.409 --> 00:21:00.190
Yeah, "Did you like this?

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.620
Wait till this happens."

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:07.399
That was my biggest problem with Age of Ultron
when it came out, was that at the time it

00:21:07.399 --> 00:21:12.870
didn't really feel like a story as much as
it felt like a bunch of vignettes explaining

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:15.100
what you could expect in the next few movies.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:16.100
Sure.

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:22.019
And I don't know, it's good advertising I
guess.

00:21:22.019 --> 00:21:23.019
I don't know.

00:21:23.019 --> 00:21:32.200
I feel like MCU is running out of great big
Avengers-y characters for us care about.

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:34.140
I would have been interested...

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:35.380
Sorry, one last thing.

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:39.519
I was just going to say, I would have been
interested if it teased Shang-Chi, if it teased

00:21:39.519 --> 00:21:44.169
The Eternals, if it teased the new Spider-Man
movie, but instead it teased a picture of

00:21:44.169 --> 00:21:45.169
Hawkeye.

00:21:45.169 --> 00:21:46.169
Fair, yeah.

00:21:46.169 --> 00:21:49.380
Well, the Oscars ended with a picture of Anthony
Hopkins recently.

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:52.870
So landing on pictures of things, very 2021
vibe.

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:53.870
I'm just saying.

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:54.990
I guess that's a good point.

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:56.940
But anyway, what were you going to say, Nina?

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:00.889
My question was going to be, do you think
that the excitement...

00:22:00.889 --> 00:22:07.269
Like me having a distaste for Hawkeye aside,
do you think that any excitement was taken

00:22:07.269 --> 00:22:12.390
out of it because we have seen the Contessa
already in a thing we were supposed to see

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:13.390
later?

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:16.200
So like this was supposed to be her Marvel
debut.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:17.960
I mean, that would have been a bigger surprise,
right?

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:21.970
If you hear somebody blowing their nose and
you look over and it's Elaine from Seinfeld.

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:29.000
If anything, I think this worked better because
I had more context as to who she is and that

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:35.659
she's a shady person, but if she had shown
up in Black Widow and I'd seen that first,

00:22:35.659 --> 00:22:36.850
I'd be even more confused.

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:40.909
I'd be even more kind of like, curious as
to what's going on.

00:22:40.909 --> 00:22:44.070
I'm just wondering if that would have been...

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:47.529
Everyone knows who that actor is, so it would
have been like a reveal.

00:22:47.529 --> 00:22:51.100
And now we're just like, "Oh, we know that
she's shady because of everything she did

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:55.080
in Falcon and the Winter Soldier.

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:56.210
Yeah.

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:58.030
I don't know.

00:22:58.030 --> 00:23:02.669
The post credit scene for me, usually the
post credit scenes are always kind of exciting

00:23:02.669 --> 00:23:03.669
to me.

00:23:03.669 --> 00:23:06.590
And then this time I was just kind of like...

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:11.000
You know what I would love to see is like
the documentary, like in universe that must've

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:15.149
come out where all of the Avengers just went
into great detail about everything that happened

00:23:15.149 --> 00:23:17.639
while they were fighting Thanos, because I
don't understand how she...

00:23:17.639 --> 00:23:21.090
Yeah, when she was like, "She was a hero,"
yeah.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:22.340
Who did Hawkeye tell...

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:23.419
Like yeah, no.

00:23:23.419 --> 00:23:25.049
So we were on purple planet, right?

00:23:25.049 --> 00:23:31.490
And then we run into the ghost of a dude from
the forties and then one of us has to die

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:33.480
or there's no magic stone.

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:34.899
So anyway.

00:23:34.899 --> 00:23:39.280
Even though they've got a drawer full of them
at the TVA headquarters.

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:45.970
As Natasha flew away at the very end in that
ship, I said to myself, "And then she died

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:47.100
on another planet."

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:53.259
And it's just kind of one of those things
where, again, I briefly touched on this.

00:23:53.259 --> 00:23:57.840
It's just kind of like one of those things
where, if Black Widow were going to get her

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:01.610
own franchise, this would be like, "Hey, this
was a pretty good first Black Widow movie.

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:03.100
Can't wait to see the next one.

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:06.720
Cause I bet it's going to be even more action
packed and full of cool spy stuff.

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:08.600
Oh no, she's dead."

00:24:08.600 --> 00:24:12.429
Well, and that's not the pandemic's fault,
that's Marvel's fault for not having faith

00:24:12.429 --> 00:24:15.340
that a Black Widow movie would do well like
years ago.

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:16.340
Yeah.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:20.820
So this definitely bums me out, and it also
really reduces a lot of the tension during

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:21.899
some scenes.

00:24:21.899 --> 00:24:26.549
I mean again, not that you ever think that
Black Widow's going to die during this movie,

00:24:26.549 --> 00:24:31.750
just outside of any context, but knowing that
she has to live long enough to die in a later

00:24:31.750 --> 00:24:34.179
movie, it really reduces the tension.

00:24:34.179 --> 00:24:35.179
When she's in that...

00:24:35.179 --> 00:24:38.120
If any of the other characters die, it won't
affect anything that happens for the next

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:39.120
five, 10 years.

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.049
Right, because we know the future already,
yeah.

00:24:41.049 --> 00:24:45.230
That's always the problem with a prequel or
an interquel or whatever you want to call

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:46.230
this.

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:47.230
So, that's definitely one of those things
that...

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:48.230
Interquel, yeah.

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:49.950
Yeah it just rolls off the tongue, interquel.

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:50.950
Yeah.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:51.950
Midquel, yeah.

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:52.950
Midquel.

